const { pactWith } = require("@pact-foundation/pact");
const { eachLike, like } = require("@pact-foundation/pact");

pactWith({ consumer: "OrderService", provider: "UserService" }, (provider) => {
  describe("User retrieval", () => {
    it("returns user data", async () => {
      // define interactions here
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale contract testing to hundreds of tenants without flakiness?
- What strategy would you use to rollback a breaking contract in production with minimal impact?

## Why This Is Asked
Multi-tenant quotas with dynamic admin updates and cross-region resilience test end-to-end behavior, reflecting production complexity.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation
- Dynamic quotas propagation
- Observability and headers
- Failure modes and graceful degradation
- Tooling: k6, Postman, Redis, etc.

## Code Example
```javascript
// k6 skeleton for tenant traffic
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const r = http.get('https://api.example.com/tenants/t1/resources');
  check(r, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate quota propagation latency across regions?
- How would you incorporate chaos tests for cache/queue outages?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes an advanced testing scenario where data consistency is eventual and involves a read-model updated by a streaming pipeline, requiring tests around read-your-writes, consistency window, multi-tenant isolation, and resilience to streaming outages.

## Key Concepts

- Eventual consistency between read-model and stream
- Read-your-writes and drift windows
- Per-tenant isolation under concurrency
- Streaming fault injection and recovery
- Contract testing across gateway and downstream services

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test sketch: verify eventual consistency
async function testReadYourWrites(tenantId, value){
  await writeMetric(tenantId, value);
  await untilTrue(async ()=> {
     const v = await readMetrics(tenantId);
     return v.includes(value);
  }, 30000);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure the real-world consistency window, and what metrics would you collect?
- How would you design roll-out and rollback strategies if drift exceeds SLA?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design comprehensive test strategies for distributed payment systems that must handle real-world complexities including idempotency, cross-service coordination, and failure scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency mechanisms for financial operations
- Cross-service correlation and distributed transaction consistency
- Real-time status delivery via SSE/WebSocket protocols
- Resilience patterns including backoff retries and circuit breakers
- Chaos engineering for downstream service failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test for idempotency handling
describe('POST /payments idempotency', () => {
  it('should return same chargeId for duplicate requests', async () => {
    const idempotencyKey = 'test-key-123';
    const request = { amount: 100, currency: 'USD' };

    const firstResponse = await request(app)
      .post('/payments')
      .set('Idempotency-Key', idempotencyKey)
      .send(request);

    const secondResponse = await request(app)
      .post('/payments')
      .set('Idempotency-Key', idempotencyKey)
      .send(request);

    expect(firstResponse.body.chargeId).toBe(secondResponse.body.chargeId);
  });
});
```
